{
  "requirement": "The `habeas` CLI MUST use `clap` to define and parse its commands and options.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Requirement:** \"The `habeas` CLI MUST use `clap` to define and parse its commands and options.\"\n\n**Implementation**\n- `Cargo.toml:55` depends on `clap` 4.6.7 with the `derive` feature.\n- `src/main.rs` imports `clap::{ArgGroup, Parser, Subcommand}`.\n- Every command and option is declared through clap derive types, each marked `{impl}` for `requirements.cli.clap`:\n  - `struct Cli` (`#[derive(Parser)]`, name `habeas`).\n  - `enum Command` has these subcommands:\n    - `Mdbook`, `Setup`, `Status`, `Info`\n    - `Author`, with its `author-evidence` alias and an `ArgGroup` requiring exactly one of subject or `--all`\n    - the hidden `Hook`\n    - `AddInput`, with its target and mode `ArgGroup`s\n  - `enum MdbookCommand` and `enum HookCommand`.\n- `fn main` calls `Cli::parse()` at the start, and it carries the `{impl}` marker too.\n- The only `[[bin]]` is `src/main.rs`, and I found no manual `env::args` parsing anywhere. So no CLI parsing or option definition sits outside clap.\n- The other `{impl}` comments inside `Command` and `main` point to different requirements that cover their own behavior. That behavior is not unspecified extra behavior of this requirement.\n\n**Tests**\n- `tests/cli.rs::clap_defines_commands_options_and_usage_errors` runs the real binary and checks:\n  - `--help` output has the clap usage line and lists every public subcommand.\n  - The hidden `hook` command stays out of help.\n  - `status --help` shows `--all` and `--verbose`.\n  - The `author-evidence` alias resolves to `author`.\n  - Clap usage errors exit with code 2 and a usage message for an unknown option, a missing required argument, and the conflicting `author x --all`.\n- `tests/commands.rs::add_input_requires_one_target_and_mode_and_resets_changed_records` covers clap's `ArgGroup` rules for `add-input`:\n  - Two targets, two modes, a missing target and a missing mode each exit with code 2 and `Usage: habeas add-input`.\n  - No evidence is written in any of those cases.\n- Together these cover the interesting cases: success paths, hidden commands, aliases, conflicts, missing arguments and unknown arguments.\n\nBoth obligations are implemented and marked completely, and the tests are adequate. I found no gaps and no FIXMEs.",
